nies or  groups a similar proportion of the  contributors to the New England  handicrafts exhibition were women the  exhibition of modern British crafts may  have contributed both negatively and  positively to the mid-century discourse  around decoration femininity and  modernism on the one hand the fact that  this was a craft exhibition and featured  many women artists may have reinforced  the association of women with the  decorative a complicated but often  devalued category in the United States  at this moment on the other hand the  exhibition foregrounded the domestic  revealed that women had been extremely  active in the interwar British crafts  revival and found them customers in  North America it also blurred boundaries  by including objects designed by male  artists whose paintings had been  displayed in Britain at war such as  Sutherland ravilious and Edward Borden  finally the exhibition may have played a  small part in nurturing the post war  craft revival that developed in the  United States local craftspeople were  encouraged to handle the objects in the  exhibition and many reported the  exhibition was that the experience was  instructive and inspiring in reinforcing  what has been called the myth of the  p artisan the exhibition not only  aligned itself with the idealism and  romanticism of the Arts and Crafts  movement but also invited interpretation  in the context of rehabilitation and  occupational therapy for wounded and off  duty servicemen a roll crafts performed  in both countries the catalogue declared  the artists and craftsmen of the United  States are now in the unenviable  position as those are great britain eat  and wrote to the british council nothing  could have been so helpful to what we  are calling the american handicraft  movement as this british exhibition it  answers with an overwhelming affirmative  the question which many of us have been  asking should we keep on in our vital  work for handicrafts countless Americans  will long be grateful to you for this  fine encouragement of a common cause to  conclude the exhibition was an assertion  of transatlantic solidarity and at the  same time it was a manifesto for the  importance of craft in the modern world  the conservatism nationalism and  nostalgia of the exhibition should not  obscure its defiant utopianism and it's  potentially radical purpose in bringing  handicraft women domesticity and the  legacy of a socialist leaning Arts and  Crafts movement to center stage in a  time of war thank  you

