ent the  curator of paintings at the mfa boston  declared this exhibition stands for the  kind of life we are fighting to preserve  the successful voyage of the objects  across hostile seas was remarked on  several times there were also a handful  of wartime exhibits among the about  among the objects including the  commemorative silver Dunkirk Cup most  importantly the exhibition was part of  the wartime picture that Britain  presented to the United States and it  needs to be understood in relation to  the broader visual culture that crossed  the Atlantic there is something jarring  about these immaculate ordered  meticulously curated interior spaces  presented against the invisible but  inevitable backdrop of the war the  exhibition displayed in roses words  domestic objects closely related to the  daily life of visitors of every sort  groups and arrangements suggesting rooms  it was an exhibition of the modern  British home back in Europe bombardment  of civilian areas brought the work war  into countless homes artists produced  poignant images that depicted a  threatened ravaged abandoned domesticity  such images would have been extremely  familiar to the American audiences who  attended the exhibition of modern  British crafts it opened at the  Metropolitan Museum of Art on May 22nd  1942 almost exactly a year before on May  twenty-third 1941 the Museum of Modern  Art had launched the exhibition Britain  at war visitors who purchased the  Britain at war catalog would have taken  home examples from Graham Sutherlands  devastation series photographs of bonds  at bomb sites and Edward our design is a  pub in silver town also in New York in  1941 bloody but unbowed pictures of  Britain under fire was published  featuring among others this scene of  relocated de master sir tea and in 1943  an issue of war pictures by British  artists published in London New York and  Toronto focused on the subject of air  raids it produced many paint reproduced  many paintings of destroyed buildings by  Sutherland John Piper and others  including business as usual by Bertram  how it Lodge framed by the debris are  incongruous remnants of middle-class  limit living spaces in business as usual  the furniture seems to have been set up  outside the front of the building  presumably because the interior is  uninhabitable recalling the displacement  captured in the photograph in a pub in  silver town the roof has been blown away  to expose the room part of which remains  in tax the furniture staying in place  despite the steeply sloping floor by  allowing the sofas to defy gravity our  designate conveys an eerie sense of dis  juncture between the irrational and  resilient interior with its immovable  furniture and the destruction around it  similarly the furniture and business as  usual seem stubbornly to resist  submitting to the surrounding chaos a  jagged line on the ground marks the  border of cleanliness and order the dust  having been swept away while at the left  edge a chest of drawers is placed at a  right angle to the wall defiantly  shutting  to the rubble from the domestic scene in  each case of Prim ornament ality clashes  with the grim dirty setting the curves  on the arms of the sofa and the armchair  the pink and white patterned wallpaper  in one and the pink and white ceramics  and the other the gold framed paintings  the only living figure in each is a  passerby a running man in one and a  cautious cat and the other leaving these  spaces uninhabited there is something  haunting about the unsettling ability of  objects and spaces to withstand complete  annihilation so that's popped so that  pockets of order and traces of art  remain meanwhile the incongruity of the  domestic reasserting itself outdoors  amid chaos defies expectations and draws  attention to the permeable borders  between public and private space john  farley president of the arts and crafts  exhibition society and a member of the  committee that organized the exhibition  of modern british crafts wrote of the  degrading exposure he witnessed at bomb  sites two nights after the bombing of  Coventry in 1940 at this moment it's  people are being dug out of the evil  heap of bricks dust and household  remains those bits and pieces that  reveal the pathos of men's lives the  sides of the house are torn away  exposing the dirty linen he describes  with some discuss to the quality of the  objects uncovered noting for example how  the wallpaper hangs down in shreds  revealing the vulgarity of preceding  generations as layer after layer of  hideous patterns come to light a few  months later he continues in a similar  vein surely there is a warning here that  the permanent materials we use should be  made worthy by the form they are given  at least this war has provided the  moment to throw into the melting pot the  vast store of brass litters lizards sham  iron columns and miles of railing that  railings that had no virtue in them  finally attempts to salvage from the  suffering and destruction a call to arms  for craftspeople and designers he  suggests in a later article that the  Englishman's home is  the most significant exhibition of the  culture of this country it is a fact  that might well give us pause to think  it is certain that we are judged as a  nation more by the home of the  individual than is generally realized  the destruction of homes caused caused  by bombardment provides an important  context for understanding the exhibition  of modern British crafts the show  presented a vision of the modern British  home for inspection it's installations  both denied and echoed the widely  circulating images of destruction  zooming out from the immersive views of  the room displays we see how abruptly  they are separated off from the gallery  space the neatly laid out clean  harmonious interior is just like a real  room except that the fourth wall has  been removed and we are able to look in  there is a striking visual analogy with  the representations of partially intact  private interiors exposed to public view  by the action of bombs the installations  call to mind the violence done to  similar rooms and objects and of course  to their inhabitants it has been  observed that in many images of the  aftermath of bombing buildings appear  distinctly anthropomorphic as Brian  Foster puts it these brooding paintings  shift death onto semi-human  architectural shells similarly Farley  saw the experience of bombardment  reflected back to him by his furniture  on being awoken by a bomb that shook the  house he recalled I looked for  reassurance in the familiar objects  around but the furniture stared back at  me hard clear and antipathetic either  the inanimate objects had become  sentient like myself or I had become  inanimate with them for there were a few  terrifying moments of identification  myself with them or they with me I could  not tell which in these circumstances  objects cease to be still unstable and  become uncanny correspondingly the  objects and rooms in the exhibition of  modern British crafts are both empty and  full of human presence  like the abandoned structures endlessly  documented by war artists these spaces  are melancholy they are echoes of the  defunct workshops where the objects were  produced one of the poignant messages of  the catalogue was the craft production  have been halted by the war silence  studios and absent makers were called to  mind by the photographs of British  craftsmen at work affixed to the outside  of the room installations some of which  were reproduced in the catalog these  images of healthy productive peaceful  labour acquire an elegiac quality in  this context for example the centerpiece  of the town dining room was a dining  table and chair set designed by eric  ravilious whose plane was lost in action  in 1942 during the exhibition's tall one  entry in the catalogue for a book  binding called the Battle of Britain  states that the inside front cover is  inlaid with a piece of plush given by  their Majesties the king and queen from  the bombed chapel at Buckingham Palace  many objects were lent by the  Goldsmith's company whose building had  been damaged by a bomb in 1941 the  aftermath was recorded in a painting by  Phyllis ginger that was reproduced and  circulated in war pictures by British  artists in 1943 the exhibits included a  cigarette box commemorating outstanding  work in the preservation of Goldsmith's  Hall during the night bombing raids on  London this and other objects lent by  Goldsmith's company attempted to replace  destruction with production much like  the exhibition as a whole as Christopher  reads important scholarship has shown  the domestic has been denigrated and  feminized in histories of modernism read  observes that as its military derived  name suggests the avant-garde imagined  itself away from home marching toward  glory on the battlefields of culture  thanks to Clement Greenberg the  avant-garde is also opposed to catch in  the discourses of modernism  alissa author argues that for greenberg  the decorative is catch quoting the  latter's comment in 1941 when the  abstract artist grows tired he becomes  an interior decorator during the war  reads point about the military  associations of the avant-garde is  particularly opposite water countless  recruits including artists and  craftspeople away from home in light of  the feminization and repression of the  domestic in histories of modernism it is  significant that the exhibition of  modern British crafts drew attention  both to the work of women and to art in  a domestic context fewer than ten  percent of the named artists in the  catalogue of the Britain at war  exhibition were women by contrast nearly  forty percent of the exhibitors named in  the catalogue of modern British crafts  show were women and the proportion of  women represented was probably much  higher than that since many exhibitors  were simply listed as companies or  groups a similar proportion of the  contributors to the New England  handicrafts exhibition were women the  exhibition of modern British crafts may  have contributed both negatively and  positively to the mid-century discourse  around decoration femininity and  modernism on the one hand the fact that  this was a craft exhibition and featured  many women artists may have reinforced  the association of women with the  decorative a complicated but often  devalued category in the United States  at this moment on the other hand the  exhibition foregrounded the domestic  revealed that women had been extremely  active in the interwar British crafts  revival and found them customers in  North America it also blurred boundaries  by including objects designed by male  artists whose paintings had been  displayed in Britain at war such as  Sutherland ravilious and Edward Borden  finally the exhibition may have played a  small part in nurturing the post war  craft revival that developed in the  United States local craftspeople were  encouraged to handle the objects in the  exhibition and many reported the  exhibition was that the experience was  instructive and inspiring in reinforcing  what has been called the myth of the  p artisan the exhibition not only  aligned itself with the idealism and  romanticism of the Arts and Crafts  movement but also invited interpretation  in the context of rehabilitation and  occupational therapy for wounded and off  duty servicemen a roll crafts performed  in both countries the catalogue declared  the artists and craftsmen of the United  States are now in the unenviable  position as those are great britain eat  and wrote to the british council nothing  could have been so helpful to what we  are calling the american handicraft  movement as this british exhibition it  answers with an overwhelming affirmative  the question which many of us have been  asking should we keep on in our vital  work for handicrafts countless Americans  will long be grateful to you for this  fine encouragement of a common cause to  conclude the exhibition was an assertion  of transatlantic solidarity and at the  same time it was a manifesto for the  importance of craft in the modern world  the conservatism nationalism and  nostalgia of the exhibition should not  obscure its defiant utopianism and it's  potentially radical purpose in bringing  handicraft women domesticity and the  legacy of a socialist leaning Arts and  Crafts movement to center stage in a  time of war thank  you
 

